n  which would look as good as it does  today now you're saying that in the war  industries things are holding well where  is business worse in the country now  well I guess we get the most complaints  and justifiably from the textile  industry and from apparel the television  and appliance industry hasn't been doing  too well these days you know if you walk  into one of these appoints towards  almost impossible to get out at any  price very recently they remove the  credit restrictions they removed  regulation w which means that anyone can  go in and charge things and pay for them  over a longer period of time do you  think that that will help the television  and appliance industry and perhaps e  apparel industry as well I think it will  help the appliance industry a little but  the real reason why appliances have not  been selling is that the fellow who  bought himself a television set a year  ago instead of waiting until he thought  he could afford it in 1952 just doesn't  buy it now in other words when the war  broke out in Korea many people rushed  out to buy scare buying scare buying and  part of the problem we faced is that  people have not been undertaking that  type of buying now and I haven't doing a  normal bar the retailer is paying now  for what they reaped back right after  the Korean War yes they are and I think  if you look at the figures you'll find  that the extra amount is selling at that  time is just about equal to the smaller  amount of selling now it's sort of  cancel it is a sort of cancel out the  public the public demand for goods is  now going down is that correct sir I  think the public demand for goods has  gone down a little whether it's going  down at this moment is difficult to say  it seems to be sort of stabilizing at a  lower level when we had a year ago and  our our audience is particularly  interested in prices now what about  prices are they on their way down well  that's fall we've had some good  hints as to what may happen prices at  retail usually are the last to fall but  I'm happy to say that it wholesale they  have declined and we've already had a  considerable number of announcements of  lower prices for appliances for clothing  I guess about everything except fresh  fruits and vegetable and rents now you  say you're happy to say that sir it in  other words do you regard it as a good  omen is good for the American people for  prices to come down what was the Huey  when you see what the dollarboyz any a  step in the direction of buying a little  bit more as a good omen in other words  sir you you feel that it is perhaps a  good sign if the wholesaler and the  retailer do not make so much profit it's  not only a question of how much profit  they make it's a question of what we  have to pay when we walk into a store  now if they sell a large volume of goods  they can make money even though they  don't make as much on each unit call a  turnover now in this period where the  war industries are holding but where  prices are coming down who are the best  paid people the best paid workers in the  American economy now well the best paid  workers generally our co-workers steel  workers construction workers Automobile  Workers they generally run anywhere from  25 to 50 cents above the average for all  manufacturing industries aren't these  the very workers will create most of the  labor strife looking for more higher  increases in pay they usually in the  forefront well now why is that sir why  are these industries are they to blame  for it who's to blame for the fact that  they they continually seek higher wages  well many of these workers are in  industries which are essential to the  war effort and when something is scarce  as laborers in those industries you go  out and try and get it in other words  these are the people that have they are  in the best bargaining position to get  higher wages well yes look at the  contrast between the textile workers who  are fighting to stay in the same place  and say steel workers who are fighting  the chump way ahead the textile industry  isn't doing too well the steel industry  move  the capacity in other words it's a it's  an effort on the part of the steel  industry to take advantage of the war  situation to get a higher wage scale for  themselves we were still workers yeah I  thinks if that's the case when there's a  shortage of workers they try to get more  when is it so plus they just don't get  more for example in 1949 they didn't get  wage increases largely because business  conditions were sort of sadly known  supply and demand working on the labor  side very definitely but don't you feel  however that the textile in the textile  industry particularly they're just  waiting for the steel issue to be  settled well it's difficult to talk for  the textile industry but from what I've  been able to find out about it they're  hoping they can get the same contracts  they had before Chris business has been  pretty bad in that area now dr. Backman  our audience has heard a great many  experts on the steel controversy now as  some of them said they were speaking for  the people some for the government we've  had this label spokesman for industry  you're perhaps could qualify as being  objective now can you how would you  simplify the present steel controversy  for our audience well I'd say one thing  we'd want to know is what is the  economic position of the steelworkers  how much money are they making compared  with other workers who was talking to  Jerry Moore well that's one aspect the  other question is how much a lower wages  increased recently well now we've had a  mr. arnel and others have talked about  to catch up that it was necessary for  the steel industry now to precipitate a  crisis so that they could catch up now  what are they catching up to well that's  something I have not been able to find  out if you look at the figures they look  something like this the steelworkers run  in the top ten or fifteen percent of  workers in terms of wages in the economy  if you go back to almost any date you  want go back to nineteen fifty-nine they  made twenty cents an hour more than all  manufacturing workers go back to the end  of the war they made twenty cents an  hour more go back to the beginning of  nineteen fifty before career they made  24 cents more you look at the figures  today they're making 24 cents more I  think what happened is this when the  steel industry and the work is agreed to  an increase last year temporarily steel  workers got up to a spread of as much  33 cents then when the other workers  caught up to steal they came back to  this 24 cents spread and that's where  they are today and if steel workers get  an increase I think it's pretty clear as  to what will happen with other workers  the others will go ahead with them well  John elois seems to be waiting aluminum  retailers you can go right down the line  all right industries now when mr. Ellis  Arnall the economic stabilizer was on  this program he said that if the steel  industry was given its price increases  it would cost the average American  family about four hundred dollars a year  because the prices of other metals and  other services other supplies would go  up along with steel which perhaps was  logical reasoning but we never did  explore the other side suppose the steel  workers are given their wage increases  what will that cost the american family  per year whether the steel industry gets  a price increase or not Americans  generally going to pay for the steel  wage increase if the steel industry gets  no price increase it will mean that the  tax collections of the government will  fall and if that increased spreads  throughout the economy that fall in tax  collections will run over five billion  dollars a year and I might add that runs  well over a hundred dollars for every  family in the country in other words  every family in the country will pay  over a hundred dollars a year for the